\chapter{The C Library: musl on Ulix}
\label{ch:libc}

Until now the user programs of the port are small and freestanding: the 59 tools of the book use the book's own library
(\texttt{ulixlib}) and the test programs use a tiny \texttt{ulib}. For real programs, and later for the C++ library, an editor, an
interpreter or a browser, the system needs the C library every Unix program expects. The choice is \emph{musl} (MIT licence): small
enough to read, written without the history of glibc, and good for static linking. musl comes as an unmodified submodule
(\texttt{third\_party/musl}, tag v1.2.6, see \texttt{third\_party/README.md}); everything on this page is our part: the system call
layer that connects musl to the kernel, the start-up information a program finds on its stack, and the memory, time and signal
calls that musl needs.

\section{Reading guide: what you need to know, and the path of one call}
\label{sec:libc:guide}

\paragraph{What a student needs.} The chapter assumes C, the idea of a system call (a controlled jump from user mode into the kernel,
chapters \ref{ch:hardware} and \ref{ch:processes}), and a rough picture of an ELF file (a program is a file with a header, segments to
load, and an entry address; the book's loader reads them, \texttt{elf.c}). It does \emph{not} assume that you know musl, Linux or the
auxiliary vector; they are explained where they first appear. What a ``C library'' is: the part of every Unix program that is not the
program's own code. It turns \texttt{printf} into a few bytes and one call to the kernel, \texttt{malloc} into \texttt{brk} or
\texttt{mmap}, \texttt{fopen} into \texttt{open}; it also contains the code that runs before \texttt{main} and after it returns.
The kernel and the library agree on one thing only, the \emph{system call interface}, and that is what this chapter is about.

\paragraph{The layers.} From the top:

\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
  program (hello.c)         calls printf, malloc, fopen, ...
     |
  musl (third_party/musl)   unmodified; turns library calls into system calls
     |   svc 0              Linux/AArch64 convention: number in x8, arguments in x0..x5
  kernel entry (vectors.S)  saves the registers, calls book_syscall
     |
  linux_syscall (linux_abi.c)   OUR part: translates the call and its arguments
     |
  the kernel of the book    syscall_read, syscall_write, the file system, the scheduler
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}

Only the box in the middle is written for this chapter. The two ends already existed (the book's kernel) or are somebody else's
(musl); the point of the design is that neither had to change.

\paragraph{The path of one call.} What happens when a program says \texttt{printf ("hi\textbackslash n")} (the program was linked
statically, so all library code is inside the file):

\begin{enumerate}
\item \texttt{printf} formats into the buffer of \texttt{stdout}; the line ends with a newline and the standard output is a terminal, so
      musl flushes at once. The flush function of musl writes with \texttt{writev} (one call for a buffer and the pending bytes).
\item The inline function \texttt{\_\_syscall3} puts 66 (the Linux/AArch64 number of \texttt{writev}) into \texttt{x8}, the
      descriptor, the address of the vector and its length into \texttt{x0} to \texttt{x2}, and executes \texttt{svc 0}.
\item The processor changes to kernel mode and jumps to the exception vector (\texttt{vectors.S}); the registers of the program are saved in a
      frame on the kernel stack (the type \texttt{context\_t}).
\item \texttt{book\_syscall} looks at the process: it was marked \texttt{ELFOSABI\_LINUX}, so the call goes to \texttt{linux\_syscall}.
\item \texttt{linux\_syscall} has a \texttt{case} for \texttt{L\_writev}; the function \texttt{linux\_iov} checks that the vector lies in
      memory of the program (``Checking pointers of the program'' below: the kernel never trusts an address it gets from a program), walks it, and calls the book's \texttt{syscall\_write}
      for each piece.
\item The book's code writes to the terminal and returns the number of bytes; the translation turns a failure into a negative Linux error
      number, puts the result into \texttt{x0} of the saved frame, and returns from the exception. The program continues after the
      \texttt{svc} with the result in \texttt{x0}.
\end{enumerate}

Every other call has the same form: a \texttt{case} in \texttt{linux\_syscall}, sometimes a single line that calls the book's function,
sometimes (memory, signals, sockets) real code in this chapter.

\paragraph{The path of the program before \texttt{main}.} \texttt{execve} loads the ELF file; the kernel builds the first stack
(\texttt{build\_linux\_stack}, chapter \ref{ch:processes}): from the stack pointer upwards there are \texttt{argc}, the pointers of
\texttt{argv}, a null pointer, the pointers of the environment, another null pointer, and the pairs of the \emph{auxiliary vector}
(\texttt{AT\_PHDR}, \texttt{AT\_PHENT}, \texttt{AT\_PHNUM}, \texttt{AT\_PAGESZ}, \texttt{AT\_RANDOM}); after that the strings themselves.
musl's start code (section ``Building musl and the first program'') reads exactly this layout. Where something in this chapter looks
arbitrary (why sixteen random bytes? why the address of the program headers?), the reason is that musl reads it there.

\paragraph{Reading order.} First this section and the next one (the decision to translate in the kernel). Then the start of the system
call layer (numbers, errors, checking pointers), \texttt{linux\_syscall} at the end of the layer (it shows the whole list at a
glance), and the build and the first program. The calls in between (memory, time, threads, signals, files, sockets) can be read
as needed: each starts with the simplest version and says what it leaves out. The later sections (\texttt{cat}, \texttt{ls}, tests,
Python) show the layer under load. To see it work, start the system with \texttt{debug=http} on the kernel command line: for the Python web server it prints
every system call with its result and duration (\texttt{ARM64-PORT.md}, section 43); the kernel also prints the number of a call that is
still missing (\texttt{ENOSYS}), once. The test programs of this chapter (\texttt{hello}, \texttt{threadtest}, \texttt{sockettest}, \ldots)
are run by the kernel test, and their output says what each line proves.

\section{How musl attaches to the kernel}

musl talks to its kernel through one inline function per number of arguments (\texttt{\_\_syscall0} to \texttt{\_\_syscall6},
\texttt{arch/aarch64/syscall\_arch.h}): the \emph{Linux/AArch64 convention} --- number in \texttt{x8}, arguments in \texttt{x0} to
\texttt{x5}, \texttt{svc 0}, result in \texttt{x0}, and an error is returned as a negative error number. Ulix has a different
convention (number in \texttt{x0}, arguments in \texttt{x1} to \texttt{x5}, result \texttt{-1} and the error number kept in the
process), with the numbers of the book, which are those of Linux/i386. The two conventions cannot be told apart from a single
\texttt{svc}: \texttt{x0} is the number in one and the first argument in the other.

Three ways were considered (the decision is recorded in issue \#27 and in \texttt{ARM64-PORT.md}):

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Change musl} (\texttt{syscall\_arch.h}) so that it uses the Ulix convention. This is the translation table ``in
  \texttt{arch/aarch64}''. It makes the submodule a fork and every musl update a merge, and the translation of structures and
  64-bit results would still be needed.
\item \emph{Renumber the kernel} to the Linux/AArch64 numbers. All existing programs, the book's \texttt{ulixlib} and the
  tests would have to be rebuilt, and the numbers of the book (which the students read) would disappear from the port.
\item \emph{A second system call interface in the kernel}, chosen per program. The kernel stays as it is for the old programs and
  speaks Linux/AArch64 to the programs that musl built. musl stays unmodified, and the translation is in one place, in our
  code, readable next to the kernel functions it calls.
\end{enumerate}

We take the third way. A program tells which interface it wants in its ELF header: the byte \texttt{EI\_OSABI} is
\texttt{ELFOSABI\_LINUX} (3). The linker does not write that byte, so the build sets it after linking
(\texttt{tools/mark\_musl.py}). The loader (\texttt{elf.c}) remembers it, \texttt{linux\_abi\_as[]} keeps it for the address space
(copied by \texttt{fork}, replaced by \texttt{execve}), and \texttt{book\_syscall} sends the call to \texttt{linux\_syscall} instead of
the book's dispatcher.

The function \texttt{linux\_syscall} is the translation: it looks at \texttt{x8}, and for most calls it moves the arguments into the
registers the book's handler (\texttt{syscall\_read}, \texttt{syscall\_write}, \ldots) expects and calls that handler. A few calls
need real work because the kernel has nothing similar yet: \texttt{mmap}, \texttt{mprotect} and friends (this chapter), the clocks,
the signal calls. What is missing is answered with \texttt{ENOSYS}, and the kernel prints the number once, so that a program that
needs more shows exactly what.

The numbers below are the Linux/AArch64 numbers as they are listed in musl's own header (\texttt{bits/syscall.h.in}); they are facts
about an interface, no Linux code is used.

