\subsection{Threads: \texttt{futex}}
\label{sec:libc:futex}

A \emph{futex} is how a library puts a thread to sleep without the kernel knowing what a lock is: \texttt{FUTEX\_WAIT (address, value)} sleeps
if the word at \texttt{address} still has the \texttt{value} (so a wake-up that came between the program's look and the call is not lost) and
\texttt{FUTEX\_WAKE (address, n)} wakes up to $n$ sleepers on that address. musl's mutexes, condition variables, \texttt{pthread\_join}
and the lock around its own thread list are built from these two calls; the kernel never sees a mutex.

\paragraph{A blocked queue, like the pipes.} A thread that waits does not stay in the ready queue. It goes into one queue for all futexes
(\texttt{futex\_queue}, state \texttt{TSTATE\_WAITFUTEX}), exactly as a reader goes into \texttt{pipe\_queue}, and it costs no CPU time until somebody
wakes it. (The first version of this chapter kept the sleeper in the ready queue and called \texttt{arch\_resign} in a loop; a waiting thread then used a whole
CPU, which \texttt{threadtest} now shows as a number.) Four things end the sleep, and the same function \texttt{futex\_sleep} serves all of them:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{FUTEX\_WAKE} finds the sleepers on the address in the order in which they went to sleep, marks them \texttt{woken} and makes them ready with \texttt{deblock}.
\item The \emph{time limit} (timed waits, \texttt{nanosleep}): every sleeper has a deadline, and the timer tick asks \texttt{futex\_timeouts} (like \texttt{key\_timeouts} for the
  keyboard) to wake those whose time has come. A timed sleep is therefore accurate to one tick (10 ms).
\item A \emph{signal} that is not blocked: \texttt{u\_kill} calls \texttt{futex\_before\_kill} first (the book knows only its own queues, as with pipes), and the
  sleeper sees the pending signal and returns \texttt{EINTR}.
\item \texttt{exit\_group} in another thread: \texttt{futex\_wake\_process} wakes every sleeper of the address space, and the same test ends the sleep with \texttt{EINTR}.
\end{itemize}
A sleeper that is woken for no reason just goes to sleep again. Wake-ups cannot get lost because all of this runs under the
kernel lock: the value is compared and the thread put in the queue without a thread switch in between.

\paragraph{Which futex is it?} A \emph{private} futex (the flag \texttt{FUTEX\_PRIVATE\_FLAG}, which musl sets for the locks inside a process) is named by the address space
and the address. A \emph{shared} futex (no flag: a lock in memory that was mapped with \texttt{MAP\_SHARED}, see the section on shared mappings) is named by the
\emph{physical address} of the word, so that two processes that map the same frame find each other. Sleepers have a \texttt{key} (the address) and \texttt{as}
(the address space, or $-1$ for a shared one). \texttt{FUTEX\_REQUEUE} and \texttt{FUTEX\_CMP\_REQUEUE} wake some of the sleepers and move the others to another
address by changing their key. (musl does not use them; a condition variable in the style of glibc does: the woken thread would find the mutex locked and
sleep again at once, so the others are moved to the mutex instead of being woken.)

\paragraph{Robust mutexes.} \texttt{set\_robust\_list} tells the kernel where the thread keeps a list of the robust mutexes it holds. When the thread ends,
\texttt{futex\_exit\_robust} walks the list: every mutex whose owner field names the dying thread gets the bit \texttt{FUTEX\_OWNER\_DIED} and one sleeper is woken, so
that \texttt{pthread\_mutex\_lock} returns \texttt{EOWNERDEAD} to the next thread instead of waiting for ever. The walk is limited to 2048 entries and checks every address.

<<linux_abi.c: futex>>=
#define FUTEX_WAIT          0
#define FUTEX_WAKE          1
#define FUTEX_REQUEUE       3
#define FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE   4
#define FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET   9
#define FUTEX_WAKE_BITSET  10
#define FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG 128
#define FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME 256
#define FUTEX_OWNER_DIED   0x40000000
#define LE_ETIMEDOUT 110
#define TSTATE_WAITFUTEX 15                   // a thread sleeps in futex_queue (12 serial disk, 13 pipe, 14 network)

extern int arch_as_dying (void);

static blocked_queue futex_queue;             // everybody who waits: futex, nanosleep, semaphores
struct futex_waiter { u64 key;  int as, woken;  unsigned long long deadline; };    // one per thread: what it waits for (key 0: nothing)
static struct futex_waiter futex_waiters[MAX_THREADS];

// the name of the futex at this address: private to the address space, or (shared) named by the physical address of the word
static void futex_name (u64 addr, int shared, u64 *key, int *as) {
  if (shared) { *key = user_to_phys (addr, 0);  *as = -1; }
  else        { *key = addr;                    *as = current_as; }
}

// a signal that can be delivered (a blocked one does not end a wait), or the end of the process
static int futex_must_leave (void) {
  TCB *t = &thread_table[current_task];
  return (t->sig_pending & ~t->sig_blocked) != 0 || arch_as_dying ();
}

// sleep until woken (0), until the deadline (mono_ns, 0: none; -ETIMEDOUT), or until a signal or exit_group (-EINTR)
static long futex_sleep (u64 key, int as, unsigned long long deadline) {
  struct futex_waiter *w = &futex_waiters[current_task];
  w->key = key;  w->as = as;  w->woken = 0;  w->deadline = deadline;
  long result;
  for (;;) {
    if (futex_must_leave ()) { result = -LE_EINTR; break; }
    if (deadline && mono_ns () >= deadline) { result = -LE_ETIMEDOUT; break; }
    block (&futex_queue, TSTATE_WAITFUTEX);
    arch_resign ();                                              // here the thread sleeps
    if (w->woken) { result = 0; break; }
  }
  w->key = 0;  w->deadline = 0;
  return result;
}

// wake up to n sleepers on this futex, oldest first; returns how many
static int futex_wake_key (u64 key, int as, int n) {
  int woken = 0;
  thread_id t = futex_queue.next;
  while (t != 0 && woken < n) {
    thread_id next = thread_table[t].next;                       // (deblock changes the links)
    if (futex_waiters[t].key == key && futex_waiters[t].as == as) { futex_waiters[t].woken = 1;  deblock (t, &futex_queue);  woken++; }
    t = next;
  }
  return woken;
}

static int futex_wake (u64 addr, int shared, int n) {
  u64 key;  int as;
  futex_name (addr, shared, &key, &as);
  return key ? futex_wake_key (key, as, n) : 0;
}

void futex_wake_address (u64 addr, int n) { futex_wake (addr, 0, n);  futex_wake (addr, 1, n); }     // (the end of a thread: arch_thread.c; private and shared waiters)

// from the timer tick: wake the sleepers whose time has come (they find out why when they run)
void futex_timeouts (void) {
  unsigned long long now = 0;
  thread_id t = futex_queue.next;
  while (t != 0) {
    thread_id next = thread_table[t].next;
    if (futex_waiters[t].deadline) {
      if (!now) now = mono_ns ();
      if (now >= futex_waiters[t].deadline) deblock (t, &futex_queue);
    }
    t = next;
  }
}

// a thread that is killed or signalled leaves the queue first (it looks at its signals when it runs again)
void futex_before_kill (int pid) {
  if (pid > 1 && pid < MAX_THREADS && thread_table[pid].used && thread_table[pid].state == TSTATE_WAITFUTEX) deblock (pid, &futex_queue);
}

// exit_group: every sleeper of the address space leaves, sees the mark and ends (arch_thread.c)
void futex_wake_process (int as) {
  thread_id t = futex_queue.next;
  while (t != 0) {
    thread_id next = thread_table[t].next;
    if (thread_table[t].addr_space == as) deblock (t, &futex_queue);
    t = next;
  }
}

// one tick (10 ms) of sleep, for the loops that still look again and again (wait4 for any child): no CPU time between two looks
static long futex_nap (void) { return futex_sleep (0, current_as, mono_ns () + 10000000ULL); }

static long futex_wait (int *addr, int val, struct ts *timeout, int absolute, int realtime, int shared) {
  if (!user_ok ((u64) addr, 4, 0) || ((u64) addr & 3)) return -LE_EFAULT;
  if (*addr != val) return -LE_EAGAIN;                           // the value changed: do not sleep
  unsigned long long deadline = 0;
  if (timeout) {
    if (!user_ok ((u64) timeout, sizeof *timeout, 0)) return -LE_EFAULT;
    unsigned long long t = timeout->sec * 1000000000ULL + timeout->nsec;
    if (absolute) deadline = t + mono_ns () - (realtime ? real_ns () : mono_ns ());      // (an absolute time of the clock that the program named, on the monotonic clock)
    else          deadline = mono_ns () + t;
    if (!deadline) deadline = 1;
  }
  u64 key;  int as;
  futex_name ((u64) addr, shared, &key, &as);
  if (!key) return -LE_EFAULT;
  return futex_sleep (key, as, deadline);
}

// wake n sleepers on addr and move up to n2 of the others to addr2: a wake of addr2 will find them
static long futex_requeue (int *addr, int n, int n2, int *addr2, int shared) {
  if (!user_ok ((u64) addr, 4, 0) || ((u64) addr & 3) || !user_ok ((u64) addr2, 4, 0) || ((u64) addr2 & 3)) return -LE_EFAULT;
  u64 key, key2;  int as, as2;
  futex_name ((u64) addr, shared, &key, &as);
  futex_name ((u64) addr2, shared, &key2, &as2);
  long done = futex_wake_key (key, as, n);
  thread_id t = futex_queue.next;
  for (int moved = 0; t != 0 && moved < n2; t = thread_table[t].next)
    if (futex_waiters[t].key == key && futex_waiters[t].as == as) { futex_waiters[t].key = key2;  futex_waiters[t].as = as2;  moved++;  done++; }
  return done;
}

// the robust list of a thread (set_robust_list): the head is {next, futex_offset, list_op_pending}; the entries are linked through their first word
static u64 robust_head[MAX_THREADS];

static long linux_set_robust_list (u64 head, u64 len) {
  if (len != 24) return -LE_EINVAL;
  robust_head[current_task] = head;
  return 0;
}

// (musl asks in pthread_mutexattr_setrobust whether the kernel knows robust lists at all)
static long linux_get_robust_list (int pid, u64 *head, u64 *len) {
  if (pid != 0 && pid != current_task) return -LE_ESRCH;
  if (!user_ok ((u64) head, 8, 1) || !user_ok ((u64) len, 8, 1)) return -LE_EFAULT;
  *head = robust_head[current_task];  *len = 24;
  return 0;
}

// the thread ends: the mutexes of its list that it still holds are marked "owner died" and one sleeper on each is woken
void futex_exit_robust (int tid) {
  u64 head = robust_head[tid];
  robust_head[tid] = 0;
  if (!head || !user_ok (head, 24, 0)) return;
  long offset = ((long *) head)[1];
  u64 entry = ((u64 *) head)[0];
  for (int n = 0; entry != head && n < 2048; n++) {
    if (!user_ok (entry, 8, 0)) return;
    u64 word = entry + offset;
    if (user_ok (word, 4, 1) && !(word & 3)) {
      unsigned *w = (unsigned *) word;
      if ((*w & 0x3fffffff) == (unsigned) tid) {
        *w = (*w & 0x80000000) | FUTEX_OWNER_DIED;               // keep "there are waiters", the owner is gone
        futex_wake_address (word, 1);
      }
    }
    entry = *(u64 *) entry;
  }
}

static long linux_futex (int *addr, int op, int val, struct ts *timeout, int *addr2, int val3) {
  int shared = !(op & FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG);
  switch (op & 127) {
    case FUTEX_WAIT:        return futex_wait (addr, val, timeout, 0, 0, shared);
    case FUTEX_WAIT_BITSET: return futex_wait (addr, val, timeout, 1, (op & FUTEX_CLOCK_REALTIME) != 0, shared);
    case FUTEX_WAKE: case FUTEX_WAKE_BITSET: return futex_wake ((u64) addr, shared, val < 0 ? 0 : val);
    case FUTEX_REQUEUE:     return futex_requeue (addr, val, (long) timeout, addr2, shared);
    case FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE:
      if (!user_ok ((u64) addr, 4, 0) || ((u64) addr & 3)) return -LE_EFAULT;
      if (*addr != val3) return -LE_EAGAIN;                      // the word changed since the caller looked: nothing is moved
      return futex_requeue (addr, val, (long) timeout, addr2, shared);
  }
  return -LE_ENOSYS;
}

